---
title: Thai panang curry
servings: 2
tags: [pork]
cuisine: thai
time: 45 minutes
source: Authentic Thai Panang Curry by Pailin Chongchitnant, Hot Thai Kitchen, https://hot-thai-kitchen.com/panang-curry
---

== Curry ==

Rub the @fish sauce{1 1/2%tsp} and @neutral oil{2%tbsp} into the @pork tenderloin{10.5%oz}(thinly sliced across the grain), separating the slices.

Reduce the @coconut milk{3/4%cup} in a #wok{} over medium-high heat until thick. Stir in the @panang curry paste{4%tbsp}, turn the heat to medium-low, and fry a few minutes, stirring, until the oil separates. Loosen with a little coconut milk if it sticks.

Add the @palm sugar{1 1/2%tbsp} and @makrut lime leaves{10}(7 torn, 3 finely shredded for garnish) and cook a minute until the sugar dissolves.

Add the pork and toss, separating the slices. When it is about half cooked, add the rest of the coconut milk and stir one more minute, until the pork is nearly done. Turn off the heat; it finishes in the residual heat. Add a splash of @water{} if it looks dry.

Stir in the @?red bell pepper{1/8}(julienned). Season with more @fish sauce{}; how much depends on the paste.

Plate, drizzle with a little more @coconut milk{} if you have it, and top with the shredded lime leaves and more pepper.

Serve with @jasmine rice{}.

> Overcooked pork turns chewy, so take it off early.

== Homemade panang paste (optional) ==

Toast the @?cumin seeds{3/4%tsp} in a dry #skillet{} over medium heat ~{2-3%minutes} until they darken and pop. Tip out, then toast the @?coriander seeds{1 1/2%tsp} the same way.

Seed the @?mild dried chilies{10%g}(such as guajillo or puya) and cut into chunks. Grind with the seeds to a powder in a spice grinder. Add the @?roasted peanuts{4%tbsp} and grind until mostly fine.

In a large #mortar and pestle{}, pound the @?lemongrass{3%tbsp}(bottom half), @?galangal{1%tbsp}(chopped), @?makrut lime zest{1 1/2%tsp}, @?cilantro roots{2}(chopped; or 6 cilantro stems) and @?coarse salt{1/2%tsp} to a fine paste.

Add the @?shallots{1/4%cup}(chopped) and @?garlic{3%tbsp}(chopped) and pound, working in the dry spices once the mix turns wet. Pound fine, then pound in the @?fermented shrimp paste{1%tsp}.

== Semi-homemade panang paste (optional) ==

Toast and grind @?cumin seeds{3/4%tsp} and @?coriander seeds{1 1/2%tsp} as above, then grind in the @?roasted peanuts{} until fine.

Mix the spices into the @?red curry paste{4%tbsp} with @?fermented shrimp paste{1%tsp}, if the curry paste has none.
